{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,16]],"date-time":"2026-08-16T21:07:31Z","timestamp":1786914451675,"version":"build-2736575974"},"reference-count":88,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"21","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,12]],"date-time":"2024-09-12T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1726099200000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["ChemCatChem"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,11]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>Oxygen Evolution Reaction (OER) is a fundamental process, with gold standards like RuO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub> and IrO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>. Recently, it was suggested that OER could go through unconventional intermediates, \u2212O\u2212H, \u2212OO\u2212H. \u2212O\u2212H is formed by the direct interaction of an adsorbed O species to a metal surface atom and a proton bound to a surface oxygen. Similarly, in \u2212OO\u2212H, an adsorbed \u2212OO adduct interacts with a proton on a surface oxygen. This work compares the nature of key intermediates of OER on TiO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>, RuO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>, and IrO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>(110) surfaces by Density Functional Theory (DFT) calculations and Ab\u2010Initio Molecular Dynamics (AIMD). We rationalized the nature, the relative stability trends in vacuum and the effect of water solvation of the species. \u2212OO\u2212H is preferred than \u2212OOH. \u2212OH is preferred than \u2212O\u2212H except for RuO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>. We investigated the nature of the catalyst\/water interfaces and the interaction of intermediates with water based on AIMD. On RuO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>, \u2212OH and \u2212O\u2212H display a different interaction with water. \u2212OO\u2212H is quite rigid on RuO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub>, while it is dynamic on IrO<jats:sub>2<\/jats:sub> as the proton is shared between \u2212OO and a surface oxygen atom. This study provides insights on the role of solvation to the nature of OER intermediates, that may help future studies.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/cctc.202400813","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2024,7,19]],"date-time":"2024-07-19T11:14:06Z","timestamp":1721387646000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":13,"title":["Stability and Solvation of Key Intermediates of Oxygen Evolution on TiO<sub>2<\/sub>, RuO<sub>2<\/sub>, IrO<sub>2<\/sub> (110) Surfaces: A Comparative DFT Study"],"prefix":"10.1002","volume":"16","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-3791-6656","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Elisabetta","family":"Inico","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Materials Science University of Milano-Bicocca  Via Cozzi 55 20125 Milano Italy"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4289-2732","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Giovanni","family":"Di Liberto","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Materials Science University of Milano-Bicocca  Via Cozzi 55 20125 Milano Italy"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6879-9424","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Livia","family":"Giordano","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Materials Science University of Milano-Bicocca  Via Cozzi 55 20125 Milano Italy"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,12]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_9_1_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/nchem.141"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1038\/s41586-019-1260-x"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1126\/science.aaf5